# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[specific]] [[system]] of [[electrical]] [[circuit]]s in a particular [[device]]; {{lb|en|uncountable}} the [[design#Noun|design]] of such a system.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ernst H[enry] Krause|authorlink=Ernst Henry Krause|title=High Altitude Research with V-2 Rockets|editor=[[w:Luther P. Eisenhart|Luther P[fahler] Eisenhart]]|journal={{w|Proceedings of the American Philosophical Society}}|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher={{w|American Philosophical Society}}{{nb...|Independence Square, Philadelphia 6}}|date=25 April 1947|volume=91|issue=5|section=figure 3 caption|page=432|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zjPVAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA432|column=2|issn=0003-049X|oclc=299393900|passage=Telemetering transmitter removed from its pressurized container. The '''circuitry''' for each of the 23 channels can be quickly unplugged for servicing.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Lew Allen, Jr.|authorlink=Lew Allen|author2=James L. Beavers, II|author3=William A. Whitaker|author4=Jasper A. Welch, Jr.|author5=Roddy B. Walton|title=Project Jason Measurement of Trapped Electrons from a Nuclear Device by Sounding Rockets|journal={{w|Journal of Geophysical Research}}|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher={{w|American Geophysical Union}}|month=August|year=1959|volume=64|issue=8|page=894|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=W0cPAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA894|column=1|issn=0148-0227|oclc=1039445258|passage=After jettison of nose cap, all the detector '''circuitry''' became exposed to the radiation environment.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Alfred G. Beswick|chapter=Impact-detection Experiment|editor=Charles T. D’Aiutolo|title=The Micrometeoroid Satellite Explorer XIII (1961 Chi): Collected Papers on Design and Performance|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:NASA|National Aeronautics and Space Administration]]|month=November|year=1964|series=NASA Technical Note|seriesvolume=D-2468|section=section II (Impact-detection-systems Operation)|pages=177–178|pageref=177|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=mHZMJYgp84MC&pg=RA7-PA177|oclc=249427912|passage=The output signal of the multi-vibrator pulse-shaper circuit was the impact-event counting pulse. Its constant parameters provide a better form of input signal for the counting and storing '''circuitries''' than the originally tranduced impact signal. The status of the impact event-counting and storage '''circuitries''' was telemetered whenever the satellite transmitted its data.}}
#* {{quote-us-patent|en|author=Wallace Keith Larkin|title=Acoustical Communications Headset|number=3,993,879|date=24 March 1975|column=1|columnurl=https://patentimages.storage.googleapis.com/e3/09/34/c5b2583829ac3d/US3993879.pdf#page=4|passage=Prior art radio and telephone communication headsets typically incorporate a microphone and receiver which require amplifier or other appropriate electronic impedance matching circuits for interface with the console, or at least electrically conductive wires to similar electronic '''circuitry''' located in the console.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=A. A. Dowd|title={{w|Steven Spielberg}} Finds Fun, and maybe even a Soul, in the Pandering Pastiche of ''[[w:Ready Player One (film)|Ready Player One]]''|url=https://film.avclub.com/steven-spielberg-finds-fun-and-maybe-even-a-soul-in-the-1824094654|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180531213806/https://film.avclub.com/steven-spielberg-finds-fun-and-maybe-even-a-soul-in-the-1824094654|archivedate=31 May 2018|work={{w|The A.V. Club}}|date=26 March 2018|passage=Before he died, James Halliday ({{w|Mark Rylance}}), the hermit-like, socially maladjusted tech genius who created The Oasis, hid an Easter egg somewhere in the '''circuitry''', scattering bread crumbs that lead to its location. Find the egg, and the mogul’s fortune—along with total control of his digital fiefdom—is yours. It’s the ultimate capitalist scavenger hunt!}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Electrical (or, ''by extension'', other) circuits considered as a [[group#Noun|group]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=William Jury|title=Green River Greets the Space Age: Work Begins on New $16,000,000 Research Center|editor=Chester Chatfield|magazine=The Boeing Magazine|location=Seattle, Wash.|publisher=Public Relations Office, [[w:Boeing|The Boeing Company]]|month=February|year=1964|volume=XXXIV|issue=2|page=11|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ctYMAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA73-IA43|column=3|oclc=1052694660|passage=In the microelectronics area, research and development efforts will concentrate on thin film '''circuitry''', packaging and solid state electronics (work with transistors and the like). [...] The savings in weight over old-fashioned '''circuitry''' are important when the cost of items in orbit is figured at $1,000 per pound.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Andrea Nye|authorlink=Andrea Nye|chapter=Master of Passion|title=The Princess and the Philosopher: Letters of [[w:Elisabeth of the Palatinate|Elisabeth of the Palatine]] to René Descartes|location=Lanham, Md.|publisher=[[w:Rowman & Littlefield|Rowman & Littlefield Publishers]]|year=1999|page=89|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_i1_kUBrsEQC&pg=PA89|isbn=978-0-8476-9264-4|passage=The source of that passion [according to {{w|René Descartes}}] is "animal spirits," a '''circuitry''' of mechanical energy—blood circulation, heartbeat, muscle tension—whose purpose is not to inform but to protect the body from harm. Although the '''circuitry''' operates automatically in animals, in men bodily energies also register on the soul so as to "incite the soul to correct and contribute to the actions which may serve to maintain the body."}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|figuratively}} The [[brain]]'s [[neural network]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=L. M. H. Larramendi|chapter=Electron Microscopic Studies of Cerebellar Interneurons|editor=[[w:Mary Brazier|Mary A[gnes] B[urnston] Brazier]]|title=The Interneuron: Proceedings of a Conference Held September, 1967:{{nb...|Sponsored by the Brain Research Institute, Univerity of California, Los Angeles}}|series=UCLA Forum in Medical Sciences|seriesvolume=no. 11|location=Berkeley; Los Angeles, Calif.|publisher={{w|University of California Press}}|year=1969|page=269|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_Dxgrm7dV0kC&pg=PA289|oclc=63478166|passage=Since [[w:Santiago Ramón y Cajal|[Santiago Ramón y] Cajal]]'s neural '''circuitry''' did not include inhibitory cells, he believed that short-axoned cells were important in maintaining a continuous and persistent activity in neural centers.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Kathleen R. Gibson|chapter=Introduction: Overlapping Neural Control of Language, Gesture and Tool-use|editors=Kathleen R. Gibson and {{w|Tim Ingold}}|title=Tools, Language and Cognition in Human Evolution|location=Cambridge, Cambridgeshire; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Cambridge University Press}}|year=1993|year_published=2004|section=part III (Connecting up the Brain)|page=187|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Cb1HMHirsBQC&pg=PA187|isbn=978-0-521-41474-6|passage=Some argue that the human brain contains structures or '''circuitry''' not found in the brains of other animals. [...] Others emphasize the massive increase in human brain size and posit that human behavior is an emergent phenomenon which reflects quantitative increases in neural processing capacity.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Rodney Douglas|author2=Christof Koch|authorlink2=Christof Koch|author3=Misha Mahowald|authorlink3=Misha Mahowald|author4=Kevan Martin|chapter=The Role of Recurrent Excitation in Neocortical Circuits|editors=Philip S. Ulinski, {{w|Edward G. Jones}}, and Alan Peters|title=Models of Cortical Circuits|series=Cerebral Cortex|seriesvolume=13|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Springer Science+Business Media|Kluwer Academic/Plenum Publishing]]|year=1999|page=254|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=naSuIUfVn5EC&pg=PA254|isbn=978-0-306-45727-2|passage=Increases in cortical volume translate largely into increases in cortical area, and beneath each area of cortex the cellular composition of the cortical laminae is rather similar. Thus, the operation of making more cortex seems to emphasize local '''circuitry'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Auke Jan Ijspeert|chapter=Synthetic Approaches to Neurobiology: Review and Case Study in the Control of Anguiliform Locomotion|editors={{w|Dario Floreano}}, {{w|Jean-Daniel Nicoud}}, and {{w|Francesco Mondada}}|title=Advances in Artificial Life: 5th European Conference, ECAL’99, Lausanne, Switzerland, September 1999: Proceedings|location=Berlin; Heidelberg|publisher=[[w:Springer Science+Business Media|Springer]]|year=1999|series=Lecture Notes in Artificial Intelligence|seriesvolume=1674|section=abstract|page=195|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=sOwRVCPs5McC&pg=PA195|isbn=978-3-540-66452-9|passage=The first experiment consists in using the evolutionary algorithm for instantiating low level parameters of a connectionist simulation of the lamprey's locomotor '''circuitry'''. The second experiment develops potential neural circuits for the swimming and trotting of the salamander; an animal whose locomotor '''circuitry''' has currently not been decoded.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Paula J. Brunton|author2=John A. Russell|chapter=Maternal Brain Adaptations in Pregnancy|editors=Toby M. Plant and Anthony J. Zeleznik|title=Knobil and Neill’s Physiology of Reproduction|edition=4th|location=London; Waltham, Mass.|publisher={{w|Academic Press}}|year=2015|volume=1|section=part VI (Pregnancy and Lactation)|page=1976|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=I1ACBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA1976|column=1|isbn=978-0-12-416594-6|passage=Possible changes in pregnancy to explain increased food intake and consequent increased energy storage have been considered in the context of the neural '''circuitry''' in the hypothalamus and brain stem that homeostatically regulates appetite, the mesolimbic '''circuitry''' regulating hedonic appetite, and the actions of circulating hormones on or in this '''circuitry'''.}}

